the representation review is a review of  how people are represented within their  council it also reviews what  what the areas are that are represented  whether or not we want community boards  how many counselors we'd like to have on  our council  and what type of systems we want for  voting so it's legislatively required we  do it every six years it's always done  the year before an election  what we do is  you you count us to put a report  together and you have to find a way to  connect with your community and find out  what it is that they want  so what we've done in invercargill in  the past and this year is we've put  together an independent panel  and the independent panel is made out of  citizens throughout invercargill and  bluff  and they are a very diverse group so we  have business people we have someone  that works with the prison system  we've got an ex-counselor and they're  ranging in age from 80 to early 30s  we've tried to get a really diverse mix  of people that connect with different  people in invercargill to get the  message out well my role on the  independent panel obviously is to meet  with people and hear their views  and  the  consultation caravan is obviously  going to be the most visible way  in which  we'll be doing that  but also we'll be taking the opportunity  to  present to other community groups  whoever wants us to come and talk to  them or groups that we're involved in  that we can  uh chat to  um  as a group  and obviously  anyone that wants to  talk to us individually is more than  welcome to approach us and share the  views  with that information we then will build  a report that will go to council  once that report goes to council and  they accept it or make modifications  based on what they've heard it will go  out for public consultation  so the public consultations will run  from the 6th of august and they'll run  for 1 month or 30 days  that's where the public gets an idea of  where they get the option to say yeah we  completely agree with that or no we  don't we actually  think that we want less counselors  but what we're hoping is through our  feedback form that's in the back of our  pamphlets available  that people will be able to tell us in  advance  what their options are that they prefer  when you're voting you have three  different options of how you vote for  your council you have at large at large  means that you get to pick from your 12  counselors from anywhere within  invercargill and bluff  and the advantage to that is that you're  able to decide maybe a counselor on the  other side of town you think has  is a better representation of who you  are and what you want for your city  a disadvantage is you may not get people  in your community or in your direct area  that actually represent your needs or  maybe you don't feel they have an  understanding of what your community  needs directly  the next form of voting you can do is  called at large and award system so it's  a mix  the mix system allows for  having one demographic area where it's  at large and then you have a smaller  area  maybe like if bluff wanted to be part of  a ward system and they decided that that  they wanted only people elected from  their area  now the disadvantage to that is that  maybe you have to tie different areas in  because under the system you have to  have a certain amount of people  to vote  so bluff's population is quite small so  in order to say add bluff in is a part  of a mixed ward system  you would have to also tie in maybe the  end of invic article to get people  enough people to make that vote happen  so it can be a bit tumultuous as far as  whether or not you actually feel you're  being fairly represented  the other option is to do an all at ward  system an outward system means that the  city is divided into areas  those areas have to compile of enough  people so that  you've got enough representation so you  have to have a minimum number of people  that the counselor is going to  represent  um  the disadvantage is you only get to pick  people who live directly in that  demographic area so  if they're not in that geographical area  you won't be able to vote for them even  if you really like them and a  disadvantage can be  that you don't like the candidates you  have to choose from and you have no  choice but to vote for them and  sometimes in those situations you find  that candidates actually aren't getting  very many votes because maybe there just  isn't anybody that they don't have the  support to back them up  yeah some of the feedback we've gotten  is that maybe glengarry need a rate  payers group or some kind of a community  board other people have suggested south  city might need their own community  group  because they are largely doing projects  now through south alive and other  programs  kennington is another area that is being  considered for the possibility of having  community group because of their  geographical location they're a little  bit out of the city and their needs tend  to be a bit different as far as what  they need for example they don't  currently get garbage and recycling  it's things like that where you need to  really assess whether or not the  community has needs glengarry is quite  active but maybe they need more of a  voice at the council  and community boards or rate payers  groups allow them to do that  living in bluff and having worked with  both the previous community board and  and the current one  would certainly say that a good  community board  is an asset to a community of interest  bluffs  a small community geographically  separated from the rest of invercargill  and has particular concerns  and issues that are unique to bluff and  there may well be other communities  that are part of the city of  invercargill  such as kennington  um  that may feel the same way so so  definitely  i would think that  communities who feel they're not being  represented adequately through the  council  as it currently stands  may wish to to have a community board or  a rate payers group and may get a  benefit from it  that would really depend on the people  in those communities whether they  are willing to  be on those things for a start  and support them and whether they're  willing to pay for them  if an area decides they're going to have  a community board what they would do is  they would be assigned a special rating  and that rating would go towards a  community board it would be funding  their members their members make a small  wage because it is quite a big job to  take on a community board  and those rates that would be increased  would only be for those areas  they don't actually come out of other  people's rates who don't live within  that area that they consider part of  that community  so with the representation review people  have the option of choosing how many  counselors they want so we currently  have 12 in the past we have had 14 but  as of the review in 1993 they changed  that  and they went down from 14 to 12. they  decided that there were too many people  in the council and that it was  ineffectual  um what you can review now is you have  the option of going up or down again the  minimum we can have is 10 the maximum 14  that's based on the amount of people  that are in invercargill and  it's a plus or minus ten percent based  on that  um  so what happens in that is people need  to decide  some people find that 12 is ineffectual  they think that they don't get enough  done for that many people and they want  to save money by not paying the  councillors so they may decide that they  want to go down to 10.  other people may feel that they're not  getting enough people to vote from and  they'd like a bigger pool so they may  prefer for it to go up to 14 thinking  that it will be more effectual for them  to have a more diverse group or more  voices at the table the majority of  feedback we've gotten is to keep it the  same people seem to be quite happy about  it  i would say about 15 percent have been  for smaller and nobody has voted for  larger  so we'll be consulting with the public  all over invercargill and bluff we'll be  out in bluff at the four square  we will be attending the farmers market  there's a poultry  show in kennington in july which we will  be going to because we're figuring we'll  get lots of people from all over  we will also be at splash palace and  we'll be in windsor  just kind of trying to get a diverse  group and be around where people are and  get their opinions  there we have an era representative that  will also be going to the maori school  in sit and she'll be doing a  presentation there and she'll be tapping  into a lot of the local maori groups and  doing presentations amongst those  folks as well  the three main things that we really  want people to consider when they're  giving us their feedback forms is  communities of interest  fair representation and effective  representation to get more information  on that we have booked the booklet  available you can get it on the council  website or as i said you can also get it  from grocery stores  it's going to be available at the  library it's available at the pool  and at the council building  you
 

